WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.190
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Brilliant, a problem solving website

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:07.320
that teaches you to think like an engineer.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:11.849
In the past few years there has been a lot
of buzz around the possibility of drone delivery

00:00:11.849 --> 00:00:13.009
services.

00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:17.180
Most think companies like Amazon will be the
first to deploy them in their distribution

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:22.430
centres, but few realize the technology is
already in use for a far greater purpose in

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:24.200
the developing world.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:28.720
More than two billion people across the world
lack access to essential medical products,

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:34.280
like blood and vaccines, due to poor quality
or even non-existent infrastructure.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.730
Last month I visited Rwanda with Sam from
Wendover Production and Joseph from Real Life

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:39.730
Lore.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:44.250
We were all struck by how modern and clean
Kigali, the nation's capital, was but just

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:48.940
a short drive outside the city the quality
of roads deteriorated quickly.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:53.380
The country has been working hard to improve
their transportation links over the past 2

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:59.530
decades, but of the 14,000 kms of roads in
Rwanda, only 2,600 kilometres are paved.

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:04.940
The rest consist of uneven dirt roads that
become incredibly difficult, if not impossible

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.350
to navigate during the countries raining season.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:08.350
[1]

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:12.360
We got a taste of this while on Safari when
our 4x4 got stuck in the mud, forcing us to

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.900
get out and push, keep in mind we were looking
for lions on this trip.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.549
Medical supplies, by nature, need to be on
hand quickly.

00:01:19.549 --> 00:01:24.540
If a mother is bleeding out during childbirth,
she cannot afford to wait 3 hours for blood

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:25.560
to arrive.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.720
Leaving people living in remote rural villages
in danger.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.840
Zipline, the real reason we visited Rwanda,
is working to solve this problem with their

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.950
fleet of autonomous drones.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:40.470
Each capable of carrying 1.6 kilograms of
medical supplies, about the weight of a three

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.170
500 millilitre blood bags.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:47.240
These amazing little drones have a TONNE of
geeky engineering design that was influenced

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.580
by Zipline’s design philosophies.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:52.560
Several unique factors influenced their designs.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.810
The first is the speed the plane needs to
get into the air.

00:01:55.810 --> 00:02:00.270
The moment an order comes in, the clock has
started and Zipline aims to have the drone

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:03.090
in the air in as short a time as possible.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:06.450
This is, after all, an emergency and seconds
count.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:10.069
Currently they are averaging just 5 minutes
from order to launch.

00:02:10.069 --> 00:02:18.000
That’s the time it takes for an order to
arrive in their on-site pharmacy to launch.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.050
Getting a plane into the air that quickly
is pretty astounding, some would struggle

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:26.160
just to get a drone out of its case and flying
in that time, and Zipline have come up with

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.330
some amazing solutions to reduce those seconds.

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:34.190
Just like a DJI Drone, Zipline’s plane needs
a GPS connection in order to fly as they are

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.200
not piloted, they are autonomous.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:39.970
If you have ever flown a drone you know it
can sometimes take a little while for the

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:42.890
drone to boot up and make a connection with
a GPS satellite.

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:48.470
So, to remove that delay, Zipline moved the
GPS circuitry from the plane to the battery.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:51.400
This means it’s always on and always connected.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:55.900
This innovation alone removed on average 10-15
minutes off the launch time.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:00.620
The battery is one of 4 pieces of the plane
that need to be assembled prior to flight.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:04.519
First the order is placed inside the drop
hatch of the fuselage, which is then placed

00:03:04.519 --> 00:03:08.300
on the launcher, the wings are then attached
followed by the battery.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:12.690
This modular design makes the plane much easier
to handle, allowing staff to easily lift it

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:13.910
into place.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:18.020
More importantly, it separates components
so if there is a problem found with the wings

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:22.640
during the pre-flight check, they are simply
swapped out without having to start the entire

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.030
assembly process over again.

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:29.240
Pre-flight checks are often a lengthy process
and Zipline have come up with some cool solutions

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:31.370
to hasten this step too.

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:34.830
Checking the flight surfaces is done with
a mobile app that connects to the launcher

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:35.830
system.

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:40.970
The launch technicians simply point the phones
camera at QR codes on each control surface,

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:44.410
sending a message to the plane to actuate
the control surface.

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:49.239
The phone then utilizes a computer vision
algorithm to make a pass or fail judgement

00:03:49.239 --> 00:03:51.190
for each control surface.

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:55.150
Once the plane is ready and assembled on the
launcher, the next time saving measure kicks

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:56.150
into gear.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:04:00.819
Rather than telling you how this works, I’m
just going to show you.

00:04:00.819 --> 00:04:02.599
-Cut to launch footage.-

00:04:02.599 --> 00:04:05.769
We spent 2 days at Zipline and this never
got old.

00:04:05.769 --> 00:04:10.150
The rail uses a pulley and an electric motor
to quickly and safely get the drone up to

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:11.150
speed.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:16.750
Accelerating the plane to its 100 kilometre
per hour cruising speed in just 0.3 seconds.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:21.359
It launches the same way every time and reliably
clears the obstacles in front of it.

00:04:21.359 --> 00:04:24.680
Take-off and landing are the most difficult
stages of a flight.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:29.409
To avoid having to land at the destination
the plane simply drops the supplies in a insulated

00:04:29.409 --> 00:04:33.440
cardboard box with a simply parachute, which
can be thrown away.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:37.699
Meaning the clinics need no infrastructure
to sign up as a client of the distribution

00:04:37.699 --> 00:04:38.699
centre.

00:04:38.699 --> 00:04:41.849
When the plane does eventually need to land,
the procedure to allow it to land with no

00:04:41.849 --> 00:04:44.180
pilot is even more incredible.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:48.110
Once again, I think it’s easier to just
show you how it works.

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:52.770
Cut to footage -
It may be difficult to keep track of everything

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:56.189
that happened there, so here is that again
in slow motion.

00:04:56.189 --> 00:05:00.319
There is a small hook at the end of the tail
boom, which will grab a wire strung between

00:05:00.319 --> 00:05:03.020
two actuated arms on either side of the capture
system.

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:07.749
It’s hard to see it in real time, but in
slo-motion to can clearly see that the arms

00:05:07.749 --> 00:05:10.240
actually raise up at the last moment to catch
the hook.

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:15.229
The plane is communicating its location in
3D space with the radio receivers next to

00:05:15.229 --> 00:05:19.620
the platform, allowing the wire to stay out
of the way until the last moment.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:22.830
Minimising any risk of collision with the
wire.

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:27.349
The capture system misses about 10% of the
time, but the plane is programmed to immediately

00:05:27.349 --> 00:05:31.770
detect a miss and throttle up it’s engines
to gain altitude and make another pass.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:37.240
An older design of this system, had a retractable
tail hook that caught an actuated wire closer

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:41.940
to the ground, which then slowed the plane
down enough for it to landed softly on an

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:43.379
inflatable pad.

00:05:43.379 --> 00:05:45.590
This system was fraught with design issues.

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:49.509
When it rained the inflatable would pool with
water, which was no problem for the water

00:05:49.509 --> 00:05:54.180
proof plane, but forced the staff to crawl
through it and lift a relatively heavy plane.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:57.169
It was neither comfortable or ergonomic.

00:05:57.169 --> 00:06:01.629
The retractable tail boom needed a motor to
control the retraction, which could fail and

00:06:01.629 --> 00:06:05.779
added weight compared to the simple metal
hook attached to the carbon fibre tail boom

00:06:05.779 --> 00:06:08.139
of the current generation plane.

00:06:08.139 --> 00:06:11.930
Factoring safety into an autonomous drone
network is another unique factor that has

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:14.339
shaped Zipline’s designs.

00:06:14.339 --> 00:06:18.719
Zipline works on a philosophy of redundancy
of parts in order to ensure safety.

00:06:18.719 --> 00:06:19.839
It has two of everything.

00:06:19.839 --> 00:06:22.839
Two motors, when it only uses one during flight.

00:06:22.839 --> 00:06:27.619
Two of every actuator, and if somehow two
of something breaks the plane will automatically

00:06:27.619 --> 00:06:29.439
deploy a parachute.

00:06:29.439 --> 00:06:31.990
Allowing it to softly touch back down to earth.

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:36.339
This procedure can also be initiated by the
control tower if a command from the air space

00:06:36.339 --> 00:06:38.289
authorities is received.

00:06:38.289 --> 00:06:43.460
As a result of this focus on safety Zipline
has had zero accidents causing injury since

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:46.740
they started service in October 2016.

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:50.569
The next design factor, which will be common
to any aeronautical design.

00:06:50.569 --> 00:06:54.259
Is the weight of the aircraft and in turn
it’s range.

00:06:54.259 --> 00:06:58.589
Zipline uses planes because quadcopters use
far more energy to fly, and just can’t get

00:06:58.589 --> 00:07:01.909
the range necessary from batteries and are
relatively slow.

00:07:01.909 --> 00:07:05.249
Map Animation:
These planes cruise at 100 km/h with a range

00:07:05.249 --> 00:07:11.189
of 160 kms, allowing the drone to serve an
80 km zone around this location.

00:07:11.189 --> 00:07:15.729
The inner skeleton consists nearly entirely
of a light weight carbon fibre composites,

00:07:15.729 --> 00:07:20.289
which is then covered in a light weight foam
shell which is easy and cheap to replace if

00:07:20.289 --> 00:07:21.709
it gets damaged.

00:07:21.709 --> 00:07:26.080
As a result, the entire fuselage weighs just
6.4 kilograms.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:29.539
The wings weigh 2 kilograms with wing span
of 3 metres.

00:07:29.539 --> 00:07:33.189
The structurally integral wing spar, that
is the beam that runs along the length of

00:07:33.189 --> 00:07:36.909
a wing, is also made from a high strength
carbon fibre composite.

00:07:36.909 --> 00:07:41.369
With the aerodynamic surfaces being formed
with high density polystyrene, and 3D printed

00:07:41.369 --> 00:07:42.509
plastics.

00:07:42.509 --> 00:07:46.459
The batteries are by far the heaviest part
of the vehicle, making up half of the total

00:07:46.459 --> 00:07:49.399
weight of the aircraft at 10 kilograms.

00:07:49.399 --> 00:07:54.759
Zipline employed lithium ions batteries with
a total capacity of 1.25 kwhs.

00:07:54.759 --> 00:08:00.919
For comparison, a Nissan Leaf has 24 kWhs
and your average Tesla has about 120.

00:08:00.919 --> 00:08:04.620
As mentioned before, these removable sections
aren’t just batteries.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:09.520
They contain the GPS circuitry, but also include
the data storage that hold the flight data

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:11.970
from all the sensors on board.

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:16.059
The moment the battery is hooked up to charge
at this charging station, the data begins

00:08:16.059 --> 00:08:18.289
to poor into Zipline’s server.

00:08:18.289 --> 00:08:22.849
For every hour of flight these drones generate
1 gigabyte of data.

00:08:22.849 --> 00:08:25.439
This for me, is Zipline’s greatest asset.

00:08:25.439 --> 00:08:30.169
These drones don’t just face engineering
challenges, but regulatory challenges too.

00:08:30.169 --> 00:08:34.719
Fitting a large autonomous network of drones
into the already busy and highly regulated

00:08:34.719 --> 00:08:38.269
American airspace would be incredibly difficult.

00:08:38.269 --> 00:08:42.710
Rwanda serves as not just a worthy cause,
but a valuable test bed for integrating a

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:46.760
network of autonomous drones into a countries
air traffic control.

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:50.790
Zipline communicates directly with Rwanda’s
central air traffic control in the Kigali

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:53.010
Airport, in the nation’s capital.

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:57.440
Having this test bed, in my mind, is more
valuable than any hardware technology Zipline

00:08:57.440 --> 00:08:58.440
has developed.

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:02.720
Ryan Oksenhorn, one of Ziplines founders,
gave us a guided tour when we arrived.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:07.149
He’s Zipline’s head of software and has
worked tirelessly to make Zipline’s autonomous

00:09:07.149 --> 00:09:09.740
drone control system the best in existence.

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:13.819
A quick look through patents attributed to
Ryan will uncover a slew of designs related

00:09:13.819 --> 00:09:20.050
to automated drone management systems, like
Patent 9997080 “Decentralized air traffic

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:25.129
management system for unmanned aerial vehicles”,
which describes software solutions to allow

00:09:25.129 --> 00:09:31.370
multiple UAVs, which have no way of detecting
other UAVs in their airspace, to avoid collisions.

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:36.540
This testbed and data is going to allow Zipline
to continue to churn out designs and concepts

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:41.339
to optimize the control of the drone delivery
network, and ultimately allow them to expand

00:09:41.339 --> 00:09:44.470
into new territories with busier airspaces.

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:48.980
This month they are expanding within Rwanda
opening a second location to serve the east

00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:50.209
of Rwanda.

00:09:50.209 --> 00:09:55.200
This could eventually grow into a supply chain,
allowing drones to hop between bases, get

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:58.730
their battery swapped out in a couple of minutes,
and be on their way once again.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:01.970
This technology is useful outside of just
developing countries.

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:06.850
It could be used in disaster relief scenarios
when roads become impassable due to flooding.

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:09.040
It also allows supplies to be centralized.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:12.620
Hospitals often have to carry more medical
supplies than they need.

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:17.889
Keeping stocks of medical supplies with short
shelf lives inevitably leads to waste.

00:10:17.889 --> 00:10:21.029
Incredibly expensive waste that costs the
taxpayer money.

00:10:21.029 --> 00:10:25.709
This is a problem Zipline could potentially
reduce by allowing centralization of an emergency

00:10:25.709 --> 00:10:29.350
supplies that can quickly be dispatched when
needed.

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:33.259
When quarantines are an issue, these drones
could minimize human exposure to contagious

00:10:33.259 --> 00:10:37.040
diseases, and so help stop the spread of disease.

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:41.329
These are both scenarios that Wendover Productions
and Real Life Lore explore in their videos

00:10:41.329 --> 00:10:42.820
released today.

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:46.439
We could not have made this trip without the
help of Brilliant, so if you would like to

00:10:46.439 --> 00:10:49.629
see more videos like this please check them
out.

